ctors and they're these tools  that basically the EPA has used since  before it was called the EPA to guess at  how much pollution is coming out of some  kinds of facilities and so those  emission factors are used in the  modeling done by these facilities and  that goes to showing the final overall  number for pollution but the emission  factors were intended to show basically  averages they were intended to be used  in a different way than they're being  used now a lot of times individual  facilities are using this information to  guess their own emissions rather than  the EPA saying in a given state there  are this many facilities and we expect  that on average this is what their  pollution would be so if you're  listening to this if someone in the  audience hears you talking about this  they say okay so data that's been really  release may not accurately reflect how  much pollution is being pumped into our  atmosphere but how does that directly  affect me can you talk about that a  little bit and the basis on which  permits for individual factories in  someone's town or nearby might have been  approved or denied based on this type of  data yeah so there are a lot of ways  that these kind of estimates can affect  individuals so for one thing your state  regulators are probably using the same  emission factors as these that were  developed on the federal level and a lot  of those actually come from industry or  are based on industry information so the  EPA and a lot of times is relying on  someone else's information to come up  with these estimates and then these  estimates are then used by your state  regulators to decide how how to regulate  the pollution and to control pollution  coming out of industrial facilities that  are in your community an occasion  or often these emission factors are  actually used by individual facilities  to show how much pollution they expect  would come out of their facility and it  is used by the state and that facility  to decide what their pollution limits  should be but sometimes that doesn't  actually match up with what their  pollution is and so there's more  pollution in that community than a  permit would be allowing for or than  anyone would have expected is there any  way to estimate at sort of like the  aggregate or national level to what  degree  we're probably under estimating the  amount of pollution that is being pumped  into our atmosphere there isn't and it's  a really complex area right like this is  a difficult thing that the EPA has tried  to get its hands around and there's a  lot of complexities to it but for some  pollution for some pollutants there's  pretty clear-cut ways to know that if  you put this much of this kind of fossil  fuel for example into this kind of  facility we can pretty much guess what's  going to come out and it should be  relatively accurate so for some  pollution we know that they're  relatively accurate for other types of  pollution like methane for example  studies continuing have been continuing  to come out the basically show these  numbers are vastly off and a lot of the  studies that have come out by that have  been funded by the Environmental Defense  Fund but have been done in coordination  with a lot of industry and companies  show that that generally means that  there's a vast underestimation of  methane so for some these pollution  estimates are actually kind of on target  for others it's just it's widely off and  underestimated and that just means that  we don't have all the information that  we necessarily need to be making the  best decisions about how to protect  people's health but also how to prevent  global warming  more global warming than what we would  like we talked about regulatory capture  pretty often on the program the idea  that sort of people from industry end up  getting into positions as regulators of  the industry that they are supposed to  be regulating and the industry sort of  exerts disproportionate amount of  control over the regulation do you have  reason to believe that that's part of  why there is so much sort of taking  industry's word for how much they're  polluting is it more a lack of resources  on the behalf of the EPA is it a  combination of the two emission factors  are really interesting because they're  really hidden in every single regulatory  decision that we see or not every single  one but many of them so a lot of times  it's really a lack of resources when the  EPA has been developing these mission  factors over time it just takes a vast  amount of resources it could take like a  year of studies and if the EPA had their  way they would be doing all of these  studies and all of this work on their  own but just because of how much how  time intensive it is and how resource  intensive it is often they have to rely  on data that other people can give them  and they try to they do try to measure  for you know what quality that data is  but you know if someone else is doing  the testing there may be ways that they  would have liked to have done it  differently and you just have less  control over the information coming in  the other aspect of that is that once  these emission factors are put in place  the EPA doesn't have a ton of resources  to go back and look and check on them so  some of these may have been in place  since the 1980s and obviously best  available information now is much  different than best available  information about air pollution and the  1980s now at the level of like the  individual activists let's say you're an  individual you learn that there's going  to be a factory coming to where you live  there's going to be a community hearing  about it you show up you express concern  about pollution the industry  representative says nope here we go here  is the amount of pollution that this is  going to put out and here's how it's  going to be mitigated you're an  individual activist and you heard this  interview and you suspect that that  information  might not be accurate what can you  really do it's hard I mean these are  really complex datasets but what I would  say is that if you have concerns about  it go to your regulators ask them to  explain to you why that information is  correct express those concerns through  regulatory comment periods you know I  think that advocates have said that they  would like to see much more activity and  scrutiny by regulators but a lot of  regulatory action is actually driven by  the squeaky wheel who's the squeaky  wheel who's actually calling for this  attention to be made to certain issues  so a few concerns you can always reach  out to your regulators and some people  have had success reaching out to  companies as well like I would think  that a lot of companies are trying in  good faith to use these factors and they  may be using them as the only  information that's there to estimate  what their emissions would be so if you  have concerns you should see them as a  resource as well all right very  important topic and a complex one we've  been speaking with Rachel Levin who's an  investigative reporter for the Center  for Public Integrity thanks for talking  to us about this Rachel great thanks for  having me
 

